1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,778
In this video, we'll learn
the gradient,

2
00:00:03,778 --> 00:00:06,731
and see how meteorology
uses it to find which

3
00:00:06,731 --> 00:00:08,175
way the air is pushed,

4
00:00:08,175 --> 00:00:12,375
and why the wind blows hardest
where the isobars crowd together.

5
00:00:13,175 --> 00:00:15,050
What is the gradient?

6
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:18,850
Let's see what it is, and why
it's built this way.

7
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,675
Then let's see what it tells us
about mountains, storms and the wind.

8
00:00:24,475 --> 00:00:26,650
Let's start with a mountain.

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,400
At every place on a map, the ground
has a height above sea level.

10
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:36,250
A quantity that has a value
at every place is called a field.

11
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:41,175
Height is a scalar field:
at each place, it's just one number.

12
00:00:41,525 --> 00:00:44,496
Temperature and pressure
are scalar fields too,

13
00:00:44,496 --> 00:00:47,025
and they're what the weather is made of.

14
00:00:47,375 --> 00:00:49,650
Today's question is simple.

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,254
Standing at one place in a field,

16
00:00:52,254 --> 00:00:55,600
which way does it increase
fastest, and how fast?

17
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,975
Imagine you're standing
on a hillside in thick fog.

18
00:01:00,325 --> 00:01:04,525
You can't see the top, but you can
feel the slope under your feet.

19
00:01:04,875 --> 00:01:07,775
Take a step east, and you climb a little.

20
00:01:08,125 --> 00:01:11,325
Take a step north, and you
climb a bit more.

21
00:01:11,675 --> 00:01:17,000
These two slopes are partial derivatives,
which we met in the derivatives movie.

22
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:20,977
Partial h by partial x is
how fast the height,

23
00:01:20,977 --> 00:01:24,525
h, changes going
east, with north held fixed.

24
00:01:24,875 --> 00:01:29,575
Partial h by partial y is how fast
it changes going north.

25
00:01:29,925 --> 00:01:31,023
On our hillside,

26
00:01:31,023 --> 00:01:35,550
let's say the ground rises thirty
metres for every kilometre east,

27
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:38,500
and forty metres
for every kilometre north.

28
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:41,400
So which way is steepest?

29
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:43,975
Let's try some directions.

30
00:01:44,325 --> 00:01:49,600
Going east, we climb thirty metres
per kilometre, and going north, forty.

31
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:52,550
What about a direction in between?

32
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:54,623
From the derivatives movie,

33
00:01:54,623 --> 00:01:58,644
a small change in height is the slope
east times the step east,

34
00:01:58,644 --> 00:02:01,325
plus the slope north times the step north.

35
00:02:01,675 --> 00:02:03,860
Walk one kilometre north-east,

36
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:07,792
and you go about
zero point seven one kilometres east,

37
00:02:07,792 --> 00:02:10,850
and zero point seven one kilometres north.

38
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:14,588
So you climb thirty times
zero point seven one,

39
00:02:14,588 --> 00:02:17,328
plus forty times zero point seven one,

40
00:02:17,328 --> 00:02:20,500
which is about forty
nine and a half metres.

41
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,525
That beats both east and north.

42
00:02:24,875 --> 00:02:27,971
Now let's turn slowly
through every direction,

43
00:02:27,971 --> 00:02:30,125
and draw the climb for each one.

44
00:02:30,475 --> 00:02:33,675
The curve rises, peaks, and falls again.

45
00:02:34,025 --> 00:02:39,200
Its peak is fifty metres per kilometre,
at fifty three degrees from east.

46
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:43,050
Now look at those two numbers.

47
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:49,050
Fifty is the square root of thirty
squared plus forty squared, by Pythagoras.

48
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:52,093
And fifty three degrees is exactly the

49
00:02:52,093 --> 00:02:55,424
direction of an arrow
that goes thirty east and

50
00:02:55,424 --> 00:02:59,424
forty north. So let's build
an arrow from the two slopes,

51
00:02:59,424 --> 00:03:03,050
with thirty as its east part
and forty as its north part.

52
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:08,125
It points the steepest way uphill, and
its length is the steepest slope.

53
00:03:08,475 --> 00:03:10,975
That arrow is called the gradient.

54
00:03:11,775 --> 00:03:13,725
Let's write it down.

55
00:03:14,075 --> 00:03:18,340
The gradient of h is written
with an upside down triangle,

56
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:21,575
called nabla, or del, and we
read it grad h.

57
00:03:21,925 --> 00:03:26,206
Grad h equals x hat times
partial h by partial x,

58
00:03:26,206 --> 00:03:29,700
plus y hat times partial h by partial y.

59
00:03:30,050 --> 00:03:34,235
Here x hat and y hat are
the unit arrows pointing east and

60
00:03:34,235 --> 00:03:36,400
north, from the vectors movie.

61
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:43,550
For our hillside, grad h is thirty x hat
plus forty y hat, in metres per kilometre.

62
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:47,150
Its length is fifty metres per kilometre.

63
00:03:48,650 --> 00:03:53,175
So the gradient turns a scalar
field into a vector at every place.

64
00:03:53,975 --> 00:03:58,825
The upside down triangle on its own
is called the del operator.

65
00:03:59,175 --> 00:04:04,050
Think of it as an arrow made
of derivatives, waiting for a field:

66
00:04:04,050 --> 00:04:08,550
x hat partial by partial x, plus y hat
partial by partial y,

67
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:10,950
plus z hat partial by partial z.

68
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,795
The third part is the change going up,

69
00:04:13,795 --> 00:04:17,800
which matters for fields that
fill the air, like temperature.

70
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:21,639
In index notation, from the vectors movie,

71
00:04:21,639 --> 00:04:26,375
the i-th component
of grad f is partial f by partial x i.

72
00:04:26,725 --> 00:04:30,955
The same del operator also builds
the divergence, the curl and

73
00:04:30,955 --> 00:04:35,800
the Laplacian, and each of them has
its own movie later in this series.

74
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:40,525
A gradient has the field's
unit, divided by a length.

75
00:04:40,875 --> 00:04:45,475
A height gradient is metres
per kilometre, which is just a slope.

76
00:04:45,825 --> 00:04:48,845
A temperature gradient
is kelvins per metre,

77
00:04:48,845 --> 00:04:53,100
though forecasters usually say
degrees per hundred kilometres.

78
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:58,000
A kelvin step is the same
size as a degree Celsius step.

79
00:04:58,350 --> 00:05:04,300
A pressure gradient is pascals per metre,
or hectopascals per hundred kilometres.

80
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:10,350
Since every place has its own gradient,
we can draw an arrow at every place.

81
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:13,100
That's called a gradient field.

82
00:05:13,450 --> 00:05:16,272
Here is the real temperature about one and

83
00:05:16,272 --> 00:05:19,900
a half kilometres up, over
Brittany in western France.

84
00:05:20,250 --> 00:05:24,796
It's six in the evening on the first
of November twenty twenty three,

85
00:05:24,796 --> 00:05:26,575
as Storm Ciarán approaches.

86
00:05:26,925 --> 00:05:30,462
These temperatures come
from ERA5, a reanalysis:

87
00:05:30,462 --> 00:05:33,704
a careful reconstruction
of the weather that

88
00:05:33,704 --> 00:05:36,725
blends observations with a weather model.

89
00:05:37,075 --> 00:05:41,101
Every arrow points the way
the temperature increases fastest,

90
00:05:41,101 --> 00:05:43,675
towards the warm air in the south-west.

91
00:05:44,025 --> 00:05:48,119
At Brest, partial
capital T by partial x is minus three

92
00:05:48,119 --> 00:05:51,227
point two degrees per hundred kilometres,

93
00:05:51,227 --> 00:05:55,700
and partial capital T by
partial y is minus two point five.

94
00:05:56,050 --> 00:05:57,071
By Pythagoras,

95
00:05:57,071 --> 00:06:03,125
grad capital T has a length of about four
point one degrees per hundred kilometres.

96
00:06:04,625 --> 00:06:06,567
It points about two hundred and

97
00:06:06,567 --> 00:06:09,700
thirty degrees from north,
towards the south-west.

98
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:14,875
Now let's see why the arrow works,
starting from a general rule.

99
00:06:15,225 --> 00:06:19,750
Suppose we walk a short distance,
d s, in some direction.

100
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:23,740
Let's describe that
direction by a unit vector,

101
00:06:23,740 --> 00:06:26,450
n hat, at an angle alpha from east.

102
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:31,750
Its east part is cosine
alpha, and its north part is sine alpha.

103
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:35,488
So the step east
is d s times cosine alpha,

104
00:06:35,488 --> 00:06:38,875
and the step north
is d s times sine alpha.

105
00:06:39,225 --> 00:06:44,149
The change in height is partial h by
partial x times the step east,

106
00:06:44,149 --> 00:06:47,750
plus partial h by
partial y times the step north.

107
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:50,950
Now divide both sides by d s.

108
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:57,206
The rate of climb along n hat is
partial h by partial x times cosine alpha,

109
00:06:57,206 --> 00:07:00,750
plus partial h by
partial y times sine alpha.

110
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:07,125
That is exactly the dot product of grad
h with n hat, from the dot product movie.

111
00:07:07,475 --> 00:07:11,611
So the rate of change in any
direction is grad h dot n hat,

112
00:07:11,611 --> 00:07:14,625
and it's called
the directional derivative.

113
00:07:14,975 --> 00:07:17,175
Let's check it going north.

114
00:07:17,525 --> 00:07:21,500
Alpha is ninety degrees,
so we get thirty times zero,

115
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:24,275
plus forty times one, which is forty.

116
00:07:25,775 --> 00:07:28,875
Why does the gradient
point the steepest way?

117
00:07:29,225 --> 00:07:33,192
The dot product has a second
form: the length of grad h,

118
00:07:33,192 --> 00:07:38,150
times the length of n hat, times the
cosine of the angle between them.

119
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:40,850
Let's call that angle theta.

120
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:42,818
N hat has length one,

121
00:07:42,818 --> 00:07:47,750
so the rate of climb is the length
of grad h times cosine theta.

122
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:53,825
A cosine is never bigger than one, and it
equals one only when theta is zero.

123
00:07:54,175 --> 00:07:58,259
So the climb is largest when
you walk along grad h itself,

124
00:07:58,259 --> 00:08:01,075
and then it equals the length of grad h.

125
00:08:01,425 --> 00:08:05,949
That's why the gradient points
the way the field increases fastest,

126
00:08:05,949 --> 00:08:08,650
and why its length is that fastest rate.

127
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,687
Walk the opposite way, where theta
is a hundred and eighty degrees,

128
00:08:13,687 --> 00:08:18,025
and the cosine is minus one:
you go down at the steepest rate.

129
00:08:18,825 --> 00:08:22,225
Now walk along a path where
the height stays the same,

130
00:08:22,225 --> 00:08:23,925
round the side of the hill.

131
00:08:24,275 --> 00:08:28,125
On a map, such paths are
drawn as contour lines.

132
00:08:28,475 --> 00:08:33,525
Along a contour, the height doesn't
change, so the rate of climb is zero.

133
00:08:33,875 --> 00:08:36,408
The length of grad h isn't zero,

134
00:08:36,408 --> 00:08:41,000
so cosine theta must be
zero, and theta is ninety degrees.

135
00:08:41,350 --> 00:08:45,800
So the gradient is always at right
angles to the contour lines.

136
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:50,531
The same goes for isotherms,
the lines of equal temperature,

137
00:08:50,531 --> 00:08:53,525
and isobars, the lines of equal pressure.

138
00:08:53,875 --> 00:08:57,552
Contour maps have a whole movie
of their own in this series,

139
00:08:57,552 --> 00:08:59,575
and so does the way they're made.

140
00:09:00,375 --> 00:09:05,675
Let's keep the hillside fixed, and send
four walkers out in different directions.

141
00:09:06,025 --> 00:09:08,625
The first walks along grad h.

142
00:09:08,975 --> 00:09:13,975
The cosine of zero is one, so she
climbs fifty metres per kilometre.

143
00:09:15,475 --> 00:09:19,725
When you walk along the gradient,
you climb at the steepest rate.

144
00:09:20,075 --> 00:09:23,050
The second walks at sixty degrees to it.

145
00:09:23,400 --> 00:09:27,075
Fifty times the cosine
of sixty is twenty five.

146
00:09:28,575 --> 00:09:33,300
At an angle, you get only the shadow
of the gradient on your direction.

147
00:09:33,650 --> 00:09:36,275
The third walks along the contour.

148
00:09:36,625 --> 00:09:39,950
Fifty times the cosine of ninety is zero.

149
00:09:41,450 --> 00:09:44,425
Along a contour, nothing changes.

150
00:09:44,775 --> 00:09:47,650
The fourth walks straight
against the gradient.

151
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,150
Fifty times minus one is minus fifty.

152
00:09:52,650 --> 00:09:56,425
Against the gradient,
you go down at the steepest rate.

153
00:09:57,225 --> 00:10:00,650
There's a quick way to read
the gradient's size off a map.

154
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:05,500
Suppose contour lines are drawn
every hundred metres of height.

155
00:10:05,850 --> 00:10:09,570
On a gentle slope, they might
be two kilometres apart,

156
00:10:09,570 --> 00:10:15,150
so the slope is a hundred metres over two
kilometres: fifty metres per kilometre.

157
00:10:16,650 --> 00:10:20,525
On a steep slope, they might
be half a kilometre apart.

158
00:10:20,875 --> 00:10:26,025
A hundred metres over half a kilometre
is two hundred metres per kilometre.

159
00:10:27,525 --> 00:10:30,975
So the closer
the lines, the bigger the gradient.

160
00:10:31,325 --> 00:10:35,608
Crowded lines mean a steep
slope, a sharp change of temperature,

161
00:10:35,608 --> 00:10:37,950
or a strong push from the pressure.

162
00:10:38,750 --> 00:10:41,283
Let's try a real mountain: Mont Blanc,

163
00:10:41,283 --> 00:10:45,550
the highest peak in the Alps, on
the border of France and Italy.

164
00:10:45,900 --> 00:10:51,223
This is the real terrain, from a global
relief data set called ETOPO,

165
00:10:51,223 --> 00:10:54,000
with satellite imagery laid over it.

166
00:10:54,350 --> 00:10:59,050
The heights are drawn stretched
two times, so the shape is easier to see.

167
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:04,700
The town of Chamonix sits at about
one thousand and thirty five metres.

168
00:11:05,050 --> 00:11:08,371
The summit, at about four
thousand eight hundred and

169
00:11:08,371 --> 00:11:11,500
six metres, is about ten
kilometres to the south.

170
00:11:11,850 --> 00:11:16,675
So on average, the ground rises about
three thousand seven hundred and

171
00:11:16,675 --> 00:11:18,950
seventy metres in ten kilometres.

172
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:24,725
That's about thirty seven and a half
metres up for every hundred metres across.

173
00:11:26,225 --> 00:11:29,175
Now let's draw grad h at every point.

174
00:11:29,525 --> 00:11:33,950
Every arrow points uphill, at right
angles to the contours.

175
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:38,027
The longest arrows sit
on the steep walls near the summit,

176
00:11:38,027 --> 00:11:41,625
where the slope on this map
reaches about sixty percent.

177
00:11:42,425 --> 00:11:44,975
Water feels the gradient too.

178
00:11:45,325 --> 00:11:50,041
Rain that falls on these slopes
runs roughly the steepest way down,

179
00:11:50,041 --> 00:11:54,475
along minus grad h, so the streams
follow the arrows backwards.

180
00:11:54,825 --> 00:11:58,975
And the size of the gradient
depends on how finely you look.

181
00:11:59,325 --> 00:12:03,147
On this map, with points less
than two kilometres apart,

182
00:12:03,147 --> 00:12:06,150
the steepest slopes are
about sixty percent.

183
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:11,312
Now average the heights over blocks
about thirty five kilometres across,

184
00:12:11,312 --> 00:12:13,050
and look at the Himalayas.

185
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:18,723
The ground climbs from the Ganges
plain, about fifty metres above sea level,

186
00:12:18,723 --> 00:12:22,575
to the Tibetan plateau,
almost five thousand metres up.

187
00:12:22,925 --> 00:12:28,250
Yet at that scale, the slope near Mount
Everest is only about five percent.

188
00:12:29,750 --> 00:12:33,175
Both numbers are right, each
for its own scale.

189
00:12:33,975 --> 00:12:36,830
The gradient works
in three dimensions too,

190
00:12:36,830 --> 00:12:40,350
and in the air, the upward
part is often the biggest.

191
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:45,518
Here is a real weather balloon,
a radiosonde, launched from Singapore,

192
00:12:45,518 --> 00:12:51,025
an island city at the southern tip of the
Malay Peninsula, close to the equator.

193
00:12:51,375 --> 00:12:54,357
It went up at half past
seven in the morning,

194
00:12:54,357 --> 00:12:58,200
local time, on the first of November,
twenty twenty-three.

195
00:12:58,550 --> 00:13:02,149
At the ground, thirty three
metres above sea level,

196
00:13:02,149 --> 00:13:05,325
it measured twenty
seven point seven degrees.

197
00:13:05,675 --> 00:13:09,746
At sixteen point two kilometres,
where the cooling stopped,

198
00:13:09,746 --> 00:13:12,575
it measured minus eighty one point three.

199
00:13:12,925 --> 00:13:18,225
That level is the tropopause, the top
of the lowest layer of the atmosphere.

200
00:13:18,575 --> 00:13:21,971
Over the tropics it sits
much higher than over Europe,

201
00:13:21,971 --> 00:13:26,625
where it's usually somewhere between
about ten and thirteen kilometres up.

202
00:13:26,975 --> 00:13:31,475
So partial capital T by partial z,
averaged over that layer,

203
00:13:31,475 --> 00:13:36,725
is minus a hundred and nine
degrees over sixteen point one kilometres.

204
00:13:37,075 --> 00:13:41,250
That's about minus six point eight
degrees per kilometre.

205
00:13:42,750 --> 00:13:47,965
Meteorologists flip the sign, and call
plus six point eight the lapse rate:

206
00:13:47,965 --> 00:13:51,025
how fast the temperature
drops as you go up.

207
00:13:51,375 --> 00:13:55,525
Above the tropopause,
the temperature starts rising again.

208
00:13:56,325 --> 00:13:59,807
Compare that with the sideways
gradient over Brest,

209
00:13:59,807 --> 00:14:02,675
about four degrees per hundred kilometres.

210
00:14:03,025 --> 00:14:06,927
Six point eight degrees
per kilometre is six hundred and

211
00:14:06,927 --> 00:14:09,575
eighty degrees per hundred kilometres.

212
00:14:09,925 --> 00:14:13,550
That's more than a hundred
and sixty times bigger.

213
00:14:15,050 --> 00:14:19,265
So surfaces of equal temperature
in the air are nearly flat,

214
00:14:19,265 --> 00:14:24,675
and grad capital T points almost straight
down, towards the warmer air below.

215
00:14:25,025 --> 00:14:29,733
In our pictures, we stretch the height
to make tilts like these visible,

216
00:14:29,733 --> 00:14:32,675
but the real atmosphere
is a very thin layer.

217
00:14:33,475 --> 00:14:36,375
So why is the gradient built this way?

218
00:14:36,725 --> 00:14:38,900
There are three good reasons.

219
00:14:39,250 --> 00:14:43,900
The first is that one arrow holds
the answer for every direction.

220
00:14:44,250 --> 00:14:48,798
Instead of a long table
of slopes, for east, north, north-east and

221
00:14:48,798 --> 00:14:52,450
so on, we keep just two numbers,
or three in the air.

222
00:14:52,800 --> 00:14:57,025
A dot product with any direction
gives the rate that way.

223
00:14:57,825 --> 00:15:02,975
The second reason is that the arrow
doesn't depend on how we draw the axes.

224
00:15:03,325 --> 00:15:06,475
Let's turn our axes by thirty degrees.

225
00:15:06,825 --> 00:15:11,500
The components change, to about
forty six and nineteen point six.

226
00:15:11,850 --> 00:15:13,707
But the length is still fifty,

227
00:15:13,707 --> 00:15:16,925
and the arrow still points
the same way up the hill.

228
00:15:18,425 --> 00:15:22,284
The steepest way up a mountain
is a fact about the mountain,

229
00:15:22,284 --> 00:15:25,500
not about our map, and the
gradient respects that.

230
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:30,250
The third reason is that
nature responds to gradients.

231
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:35,025
Heat flows from warm to cold, down
the temperature gradient.

232
00:15:35,375 --> 00:15:38,100
Water runs down the height gradient.

233
00:15:38,450 --> 00:15:42,066
And air is pushed from high
pressure towards low pressure,

234
00:15:42,066 --> 00:15:43,750
down the pressure gradient.

235
00:15:44,100 --> 00:15:49,725
In each case, it's the gradient, not
the value itself, that drives the motion.

236
00:15:50,075 --> 00:15:54,901
A room that's equally warm everywhere
has no flow of heat inside it,

237
00:15:54,901 --> 00:15:56,250
however warm it is.

238
00:15:57,050 --> 00:15:59,983
Before we use the gradient
on weather maps,

239
00:15:59,983 --> 00:16:02,575
there's one twist: the Earth is round.

240
00:16:02,925 --> 00:16:06,053
Weather data come
on a grid of latitude and

241
00:16:06,053 --> 00:16:09,400
longitude, as we saw
in the coordinates movie.

242
00:16:09,750 --> 00:16:15,675
A step north by a small angle, d phi,
covers the Earth's radius times d phi.

243
00:16:16,025 --> 00:16:19,097
But a step east by a small
angle, d lambda,

244
00:16:19,097 --> 00:16:23,027
covers only the radius times cosine
phi times d lambda,

245
00:16:23,027 --> 00:16:27,100
because the circles of latitude
shrink towards the poles.

246
00:16:27,450 --> 00:16:32,233
So the slope going east
is one over the radius times cosine phi,

247
00:16:32,233 --> 00:16:34,625
times partial by partial lambda.

248
00:16:34,975 --> 00:16:40,725
And the slope going north is one over
the radius, times partial by partial phi.

249
00:16:41,075 --> 00:16:45,725
Together with the change going up, these
make the gradient on the sphere.

250
00:16:46,525 --> 00:16:49,700
Let's see why that
cosine matters, at Brest.

251
00:16:50,050 --> 00:16:54,575
ERA5 gives the temperature
every quarter of a degree.

252
00:16:54,925 --> 00:16:59,582
One grid point east of Brest, it
was four point five one degrees,

253
00:16:59,582 --> 00:17:02,950
and one point west, it
was five point six nine.

254
00:17:03,300 --> 00:17:04,631
At Brest's latitude,

255
00:17:04,631 --> 00:17:09,224
a quarter of a degree of longitude
is eighteen point four kilometres,

256
00:17:09,224 --> 00:17:13,350
so the two points are thirty
six point eight kilometres apart.

257
00:17:13,700 --> 00:17:17,273
The slope going east
is minus one point one eight

258
00:17:17,273 --> 00:17:20,700
degrees over thirty
six point eight kilometres.

259
00:17:21,050 --> 00:17:25,225
That's minus three point two
degrees per hundred kilometres.

260
00:17:26,725 --> 00:17:31,628
Forget the cosine, and you'd use twenty
seven point eight kilometres per step,

261
00:17:31,628 --> 00:17:33,325
as if you were going north.

262
00:17:33,675 --> 00:17:37,500
Then you'd get
minus two point one, a third too small.

263
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:44,600
Near Svalbard, at seventy eight degrees
north, it would be five times too small.

264
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:50,175
Now let's use the gradient where it
matters most in the weather: pressure.

265
00:17:50,525 --> 00:17:54,924
Pressure is the push of the air's
molecules on every surface,

266
00:17:54,924 --> 00:17:56,150
a force per area.

267
00:17:56,500 --> 00:17:59,101
At sea level it's about a thousand and

268
00:17:59,101 --> 00:18:03,275
thirteen hectopascals, as we
saw in the weather basics movie.

269
00:18:03,625 --> 00:18:09,050
Lines that join places with the same
sea-level pressure are called isobars.

270
00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:12,575
But pressure on its own
doesn't move the air.

271
00:18:12,925 --> 00:18:14,750
Let's see what does.

272
00:18:15,550 --> 00:18:19,950
Picture a small box
of air, d x long from west to east.

273
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:22,952
The air on its west side pushes it east,

274
00:18:22,952 --> 00:18:26,400
with the pressure
there times the area of that face.

275
00:18:26,750 --> 00:18:29,452
The air on its east side pushes it west,

276
00:18:29,452 --> 00:18:32,425
with the pressure
there times the same area.

277
00:18:32,775 --> 00:18:36,088
If the two pressures are
equal, the pushes cancel,

278
00:18:36,088 --> 00:18:39,400
and nothing happens,
however high the pressure is.

279
00:18:39,750 --> 00:18:43,075
Now suppose the pressure
rises towards the east.

280
00:18:43,425 --> 00:18:46,867
By the Taylor series, from
the derivatives movie,

281
00:18:46,867 --> 00:18:52,275
the pressure on the east face is bigger
by partial p by partial x, times d x.

282
00:18:52,625 --> 00:18:56,574
So the net push is minus
partial p by partial x,

283
00:18:56,574 --> 00:18:59,700
times d x, times the area of the face.

284
00:19:00,050 --> 00:19:03,925
And d x times that area
is the volume of the box.

285
00:19:04,725 --> 00:19:09,254
To turn a force into an acceleration,
we divide by the mass,

286
00:19:09,254 --> 00:19:12,575
which is the density,
rho, times the volume.

287
00:19:12,925 --> 00:19:17,420
The volume cancels, and the push
per kilogram, going east,

288
00:19:17,420 --> 00:19:21,450
is minus one over rho, times
partial p by partial x.

289
00:19:21,800 --> 00:19:25,100
The same happens going
north, and going up.

290
00:19:25,450 --> 00:19:31,050
Together, the force per kilogram
is minus one over rho, times grad p.

291
00:19:31,400 --> 00:19:34,150
It's called the pressure gradient force.

292
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:38,447
It appears in the equation
that tells the air how to move,

293
00:19:38,447 --> 00:19:41,850
next to the Coriolis effect,
gravity and friction.

294
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:47,875
Only differences in pressure push, which
is why its name carries the word gradient.

295
00:19:48,675 --> 00:19:53,750
Grad p points the way pressure increases
fastest, towards high pressure.

296
00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:56,275
The minus sign flips it.

297
00:19:56,625 --> 00:20:01,305
So the pressure gradient force points
from high pressure to low pressure,

298
00:20:01,305 --> 00:20:03,100
straight across the isobars.

299
00:20:03,450 --> 00:20:05,800
Let's put in easy numbers.

300
00:20:06,150 --> 00:20:12,025
Suppose the pressure changes by one
hectopascal every hundred kilometres.

301
00:20:12,375 --> 00:20:15,201
One hectopascal is a hundred pascals,

302
00:20:15,201 --> 00:20:19,325
and a hundred kilometres
is a hundred thousand metres.

303
00:20:19,675 --> 00:20:23,575
So the gradient is a thousandth
of a pascal per metre.

304
00:20:25,075 --> 00:20:28,828
With an air density of about
one kilogram per cubic metre,

305
00:20:28,828 --> 00:20:32,775
the push is about a thousandth
of a metre per second squared.

306
00:20:34,275 --> 00:20:36,849
With the real density near the ground,

307
00:20:36,849 --> 00:20:40,100
one point two, it's about
eight ten-thousandths.

308
00:20:40,450 --> 00:20:42,953
That's small, but kept up for an hour,

309
00:20:42,953 --> 00:20:46,575
it would add about three
metres per second to the wind.

310
00:20:48,075 --> 00:20:49,975
Now a real storm.

311
00:20:50,325 --> 00:20:53,513
Here is Storm Ciarán
at three in the morning

312
00:20:53,513 --> 00:20:56,847
on the second of November
twenty twenty three,

313
00:20:56,847 --> 00:21:00,325
from ERA5, with isobars
every four hectopascals.

314
00:21:00,675 --> 00:21:04,864
Its centre, at about nine hundred
and fifty four hectopascals,

315
00:21:04,864 --> 00:21:09,525
is crossing the Channel, about two
hundred kilometres north of Brest.

316
00:21:09,875 --> 00:21:14,750
Over Brest, the isobars are only
thirty one kilometres apart.

317
00:21:15,100 --> 00:21:18,484
Four hectopascals
in thirty one kilometres is

318
00:21:18,484 --> 00:21:22,319
about thirteen hectopascals
per hundred kilometres,

319
00:21:22,319 --> 00:21:24,725
thirteen times our easy example.

320
00:21:26,225 --> 00:21:30,050
Grad p points south-south-west,
away from the low.

321
00:21:30,400 --> 00:21:35,100
So the pressure gradient force points
north-north-east, towards the low.

322
00:21:35,450 --> 00:21:39,132
Divide the gradient by the density,
one point two five,

323
00:21:39,132 --> 00:21:43,550
and the push is about one hundredth
of a metre per second squared.

324
00:21:45,050 --> 00:21:46,561
If nothing else acted,

325
00:21:46,561 --> 00:21:52,125
that would add about thirty seven metres
per second to the wind in a single hour.

326
00:21:53,625 --> 00:21:58,575
And this is why winds are strongest
where the isobars crowd together.

327
00:21:58,925 --> 00:22:01,987
Let's take every point of this
map over the sea,

328
00:22:01,987 --> 00:22:04,985
sort them by the size
of the pressure gradient,

329
00:22:04,985 --> 00:22:08,175
and average the wind ten
metres above the surface.

330
00:22:08,525 --> 00:22:13,216
Where the gradient is below two
hectopascals per hundred kilometres,

331
00:22:13,216 --> 00:22:15,975
the wind averages ten metres per second.

332
00:22:16,325 --> 00:22:20,475
Where it's above ten, the wind
averages about twenty six.

333
00:22:21,975 --> 00:22:27,152
At Brest airport that same hour, a gust
of eighty four knots was measured,

334
00:22:27,152 --> 00:22:30,650
about a hundred and fifty
six kilometres per hour.

335
00:22:32,150 --> 00:22:35,375
More than a million homes
in France lost power.

336
00:22:36,175 --> 00:22:38,300
But look at the wind arrows.

337
00:22:38,650 --> 00:22:41,225
They don't blow straight from high to low.

338
00:22:41,575 --> 00:22:44,200
They blow almost along the isobars,

339
00:22:44,200 --> 00:22:47,800
anticlockwise round
the low, as seen from above.

340
00:22:48,150 --> 00:22:50,070
That's because the Earth turns,

341
00:22:50,070 --> 00:22:55,150
and the Coriolis effect pushes moving air
to the right in the northern hemisphere.

342
00:22:55,500 --> 00:22:56,976
Away from the ground,

343
00:22:56,976 --> 00:23:01,897
the Coriolis push grows until it
balances the pressure gradient force,

344
00:23:01,897 --> 00:23:04,850
and then the wind blows along the isobars.

345
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:07,311
The bigger the pressure gradient,

346
00:23:07,311 --> 00:23:11,150
the faster the wind must blow
for the two pushes to balance.

347
00:23:11,500 --> 00:23:15,825
That's the deeper reason crowded
isobars mean strong winds.

348
00:23:16,175 --> 00:23:19,028
Near the ground, friction slows the wind,

349
00:23:19,028 --> 00:23:22,925
and lets it cross the isobars a little,
towards the low.

350
00:23:23,275 --> 00:23:27,804
We built all of this in the cross
product movie on our spinning Earth,

351
00:23:27,804 --> 00:23:30,650
and a later Coriolis
movie takes it further.

352
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:36,468
One warning: meteorologists also say
gradient wind for a different idea,

353
00:23:36,468 --> 00:23:41,025
a balance round curved isobars,
which has its own movie too.

354
00:23:41,825 --> 00:23:46,436
Here is a more recent chart:
six in the evening, universal time,

355
00:23:46,436 --> 00:23:52,200
on the twenty seventh of October twenty
twenty six, from the American GFS model.

356
00:23:52,550 --> 00:23:55,377
A deep low of about nine hundred and

357
00:23:55,377 --> 00:23:59,225
sixty one hectopascals
sits just east of Iceland.

358
00:23:59,575 --> 00:24:02,096
Where its isobars crowd over the sea,

359
00:24:02,096 --> 00:24:07,274
the pressure gradient reaches about ten
hectopascals per hundred kilometres,

360
00:24:07,274 --> 00:24:09,250
close to Ciarán's over Brest.

361
00:24:09,600 --> 00:24:14,750
There, the winds near the surface reach
about twenty five metres per second.

362
00:24:15,550 --> 00:24:19,475
Pressure has a gradient going
up too, and it's enormous.

363
00:24:19,825 --> 00:24:24,186
The Singapore balloon found
the pressure falling by about zero

364
00:24:24,186 --> 00:24:28,898
point one one hectopascals for every
metre it rose near the ground:

365
00:24:28,898 --> 00:24:31,500
one hectopascal in about nine metres.

366
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:38,485
Ciarán's crowded isobars needed thirty
one kilometres for four hectopascals,

367
00:24:38,485 --> 00:24:41,950
which is about eight
kilometres per hectopascal.

368
00:24:42,300 --> 00:24:46,256
So the upward pressure gradient
was about eight hundred and

369
00:24:46,256 --> 00:24:49,475
fifty times stronger than
Ciarán's sideways one.

370
00:24:49,825 --> 00:24:54,200
On an ordinary day, it's about
ten thousand times stronger.

371
00:24:55,700 --> 00:24:58,600
So why doesn't it blast
the air into space?

372
00:24:58,950 --> 00:25:02,000
Because gravity pulls down just as hard.

373
00:25:02,350 --> 00:25:06,936
This is hydrostatic balance,
from the weather basics movie:

374
00:25:06,936 --> 00:25:10,200
partial p by partial z equals minus rho g.

375
00:25:10,550 --> 00:25:12,509
Near the ground in Singapore,

376
00:25:12,509 --> 00:25:16,494
rho is about one point one seven
kilograms per cubic metre,

377
00:25:16,494 --> 00:25:18,521
and g is nine point eight one,

378
00:25:18,521 --> 00:25:22,033
so that's about eleven
and a half pascals per metre,

379
00:25:22,033 --> 00:25:24,600
close to the balloon's ten point nine.

380
00:25:26,100 --> 00:25:29,399
So the upward push
is almost exactly cancelled,

381
00:25:29,399 --> 00:25:33,050
and the weather is driven
by the tiny sideways part.

382
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:36,633
Because pressure falls
so steadily with height,

383
00:25:36,633 --> 00:25:41,174
meteorologists often use pressure
itself as a vertical coordinate,

384
00:25:41,174 --> 00:25:42,825
which has its own movie.

385
00:25:43,625 --> 00:25:45,750
Now back to temperature.

386
00:25:46,100 --> 00:25:50,291
Here again is the map of temperature
one and a half kilometres up,

387
00:25:50,291 --> 00:25:53,275
at six in the evening
on the first of November.

388
00:25:53,625 --> 00:25:58,975
Over most of the map, the gradient is
about one degree per hundred kilometres.

389
00:25:59,325 --> 00:26:03,875
But in one narrow band near
the storm, it reaches about eleven.

390
00:26:05,375 --> 00:26:09,766
That band is a front, a boundary
between a warm air mass and

391
00:26:09,766 --> 00:26:13,425
a cold one, as we saw
in the weather basics movie.

392
00:26:13,775 --> 00:26:18,268
At a front, the temperature gradient
is squeezed into a narrow zone,

393
00:26:18,268 --> 00:26:21,175
more than ten times stronger
than around it.

394
00:26:21,525 --> 00:26:25,825
All its arrows point across
the front, towards the warm side.

395
00:26:26,625 --> 00:26:29,425
A front isn't just a line on a map.

396
00:26:29,775 --> 00:26:32,880
Let's stack four temperature
maps above a strong

397
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:35,596
cold front over the central United States,

398
00:26:35,596 --> 00:26:38,895
on the twenty second
of December twenty twenty two,

399
00:26:38,895 --> 00:26:42,000
from about one and a half
to nine kilometres up.

400
00:26:42,350 --> 00:26:48,475
On every layer, the orange arrows are grad
capital T, pointing towards warmer air.

401
00:26:48,825 --> 00:26:52,237
Where the isotherms
crowd, the arrows are longest,

402
00:26:52,237 --> 00:26:54,625
and that's the front on that layer.

403
00:26:54,975 --> 00:26:59,550
Let's circle the stack from outside,
from the bottom layer to the top.

404
00:26:59,900 --> 00:27:03,035
Every layer above the bottom
one has its crowded

405
00:27:03,035 --> 00:27:05,779
band on the cold side of the bottom front,

406
00:27:05,779 --> 00:27:08,326
between about two hundred and sixty and

407
00:27:08,326 --> 00:27:11,200
four hundred and fifty
kilometres behind it.

408
00:27:12,700 --> 00:27:17,172
So a front is a sloping surface
that leans back over the cold air,

409
00:27:17,172 --> 00:27:19,950
and the gradient finds it on every layer.

410
00:27:20,750 --> 00:27:26,425
The gradient also tells us how the wind
changes the temperature at a fixed place.

411
00:27:26,775 --> 00:27:28,381
Over Brest at that hour,

412
00:27:28,381 --> 00:27:32,530
the wind at that height blew
at thirty four metres per second,

413
00:27:32,530 --> 00:27:37,950
from just west of south, at a hundred and
thirty eight degrees to grad capital T.

414
00:27:38,300 --> 00:27:39,592
So along the wind,

415
00:27:39,592 --> 00:27:44,400
the temperature changes at grad
capital T dot the wind's direction.

416
00:27:44,750 --> 00:27:49,784
That's four point one times the cosine
of a hundred and thirty eight degrees,

417
00:27:49,784 --> 00:27:53,575
which is about minus three
degrees per hundred kilometres.

418
00:27:55,075 --> 00:27:59,175
It's negative because the wind
blows towards colder air.

419
00:27:59,525 --> 00:28:04,375
Times the wind speed, that's
minus three point seven degrees per hour.

420
00:28:05,875 --> 00:28:11,850
This is v dot grad capital T, the
advection term from the dot product movie.

421
00:28:12,200 --> 00:28:15,454
Because the air keeps
arriving from the warmer side,

422
00:28:15,454 --> 00:28:18,458
a fixed thermometer
at Brest would warm by about

423
00:28:18,458 --> 00:28:21,650
three point seven degrees
per hour from this alone.

424
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:25,975
The material derivative
movie takes this apart fully.

425
00:28:26,775 --> 00:28:31,975
Some of the sharpest temperature gradients
form on sunny days, along coasts.

426
00:28:32,325 --> 00:28:35,696
Here is the coast of Normandy,
in northern France,

427
00:28:35,696 --> 00:28:40,550
at one in the afternoon on the twenty
fourth of June twenty twenty four.

428
00:28:40,900 --> 00:28:45,387
The satellite picture shows small
fair-weather clouds over the land,

429
00:28:45,387 --> 00:28:47,300
and clear skies over the sea.

430
00:28:47,650 --> 00:28:48,133
Inland,

431
00:28:48,133 --> 00:28:53,175
the air two metres above the ground
was at twenty four point six degrees.

432
00:28:53,525 --> 00:28:57,704
Over the Channel, about fifty
five kilometres to the north,

433
00:28:57,704 --> 00:28:59,475
it was sixteen point one.

434
00:28:59,825 --> 00:29:03,192
That's about fifteen degrees
per hundred kilometres,

435
00:29:03,192 --> 00:29:05,200
sharper than the storm's front.

436
00:29:06,700 --> 00:29:10,325
Land warms faster in the sun
than the sea does.

437
00:29:10,675 --> 00:29:13,573
The warm air over the land
expands and rises,

438
00:29:13,573 --> 00:29:16,213
so near the ground the pressure ends up a

439
00:29:16,213 --> 00:29:19,175
little higher over the sea
than over the land.

440
00:29:19,525 --> 00:29:23,725
That small pressure gradient
pushes cooler sea air inland.

441
00:29:24,075 --> 00:29:26,150
This is the sea breeze.

442
00:29:26,950 --> 00:29:31,150
High up, temperature gradients
and the jet stream go together.

443
00:29:31,500 --> 00:29:34,847
Here is the average
for September twenty twenty six,

444
00:29:34,847 --> 00:29:39,675
all the way round the Earth, as a slice
from the equator to the North Pole.

445
00:29:40,025 --> 00:29:45,047
The temperature drops fastest towards
the pole near forty five degrees north,

446
00:29:45,047 --> 00:29:47,982
by about six degrees
per thousand kilometres,

447
00:29:47,982 --> 00:29:51,700
averaged from about one
and a half to nine kilometres up.

448
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:56,696
Right above that zone, about
twelve kilometres up,

449
00:29:56,696 --> 00:30:02,150
the westerly wind is strongest, about
twenty six metres per second on average.

450
00:30:03,650 --> 00:30:06,400
Single days can be much stronger.

451
00:30:06,750 --> 00:30:10,287
On the twenty eighth
of October twenty twenty six,

452
00:30:10,287 --> 00:30:13,470
the GFS analysis shows
the jet over the North

453
00:30:13,470 --> 00:30:16,300
Atlantic at sixty six metres per second.

454
00:30:16,650 --> 00:30:20,725
That's about two hundred
and forty kilometres per hour.

455
00:30:22,225 --> 00:30:25,234
Why winds grow with height
above a temperature

456
00:30:25,234 --> 00:30:27,589
gradient is called the thermal wind,

457
00:30:27,589 --> 00:30:29,225
and it has its own movie.

458
00:30:30,025 --> 00:30:33,000
The ocean has temperature gradients too.

459
00:30:33,350 --> 00:30:35,796
Off the east coast of the United States,

460
00:30:35,796 --> 00:30:40,382
the Gulf Stream carries warm water
from the tropics towards the north-east,

461
00:30:40,382 --> 00:30:42,950
right next to colder water from the north.

462
00:30:43,300 --> 00:30:48,101
Along its northern edge, on the twenty
ninth of October twenty twenty six,

463
00:30:48,101 --> 00:30:51,475
the sea-surface temperature
dropped by about six and

464
00:30:51,475 --> 00:30:54,525
a half degrees in about
thirty five kilometres.

465
00:30:54,875 --> 00:30:59,475
That's one of the sharpest temperature
gradients in the open ocean.

466
00:30:59,825 --> 00:31:05,625
Winter storms often deepen fastest near
it, so forecasters watch it closely.

467
00:31:05,975 --> 00:31:10,550
The great ocean currents have their
own movies later in this series.

468
00:31:11,350 --> 00:31:15,178
To sum up, a scalar field
has a value at every place,

469
00:31:15,178 --> 00:31:17,850
like height, temperature or pressure.

470
00:31:18,200 --> 00:31:23,174
Its gradient, grad f, is the arrow
built from its partial derivatives,

471
00:31:23,174 --> 00:31:24,950
going east, north and up.

472
00:31:25,300 --> 00:31:28,596
It points the way the field
increases fastest,

473
00:31:28,596 --> 00:31:31,175
and its length is that fastest rate.

474
00:31:31,525 --> 00:31:35,243
It's at right angles
to contour lines, isotherms and

475
00:31:35,243 --> 00:31:38,675
isobars, and it's big
where they crowd together.

476
00:31:39,025 --> 00:31:44,500
The rate in any direction is grad
f dot n hat, the directional derivative.

477
00:31:44,850 --> 00:31:46,208
On the round Earth,

478
00:31:46,208 --> 00:31:50,925
the slope going east carries
one over the radius times cosine phi.

479
00:31:51,275 --> 00:31:55,734
The pressure gradient
force, minus one over rho times grad p,

480
00:31:55,734 --> 00:31:58,439
pushes air from high to low pressure,

481
00:31:58,439 --> 00:32:01,875
which is why crowded
isobars mean strong winds.

482
00:32:02,225 --> 00:32:05,873
Sharp temperature gradients
mark fronts and sea breezes,

483
00:32:05,873 --> 00:32:09,325
and the jet stream blows
above the strongest of them.

484
00:32:10,125 --> 00:32:14,283
This video is one of a series
of related maths tutorials,

485
00:32:14,283 --> 00:32:16,325
so watch out for the others.

486
00:32:16,675 --> 00:32:21,072
They're made for students
of atmospheric science, meteorology and

487
00:32:21,072 --> 00:32:24,725
fluid dynamics, and anyone
else who needs these ideas.

488
00:32:25,075 --> 00:32:29,847
The notebook for the gradient, and the
notebooks for the other maths concepts,

489
00:32:29,847 --> 00:32:32,600
are on the website that
goes with this video.
